          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"rostand-cyrano-de-bergerac-ad99728643cf54d1\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.96,\"analysis\":\"Christian's negative sentence is a rhetorical scalar correction: in the sequence “Je t'aime !” / “je ne t'aime pas !” / “Je t'adore !”, it rejects “love” only to replace it with the purportedly stronger “adore.” Its pragmatic force is therefore a truth-conditional avowal of exceptionally strong love, not a genuine retraction. It undertakes no relational commitment, and the quick, emphatic delivery does not independently establish expressive/reflexive force.\",\"ambiguity\":\"In isolation the sentence literally denies love, but the immediately following “Je t'adore !” strongly resolves it as an intensified avowal rather than a sincere denial.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Christian directly addresses Roxane; the highlighted negative avowal forms the middle of his spoken rhetorical correction from loving to adoring her.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Roxane has grown impatient with Christian's repetitive, ineloquent declarations. As she starts to leave, he hurriedly varies the formula by denying the weaker term and substituting “I adore you,” hoping intensification will retain her interest. Roxane understands that he loves her but rejects his lack of eloquence. The line is comically unsuccessful rhetoric, not evidence that Christian lacks loving or desirous feelings.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“CHRISTIAN (vivement, la retenant): Non ! je ne t'aime pas !”\",\"supports\":\"The utterance is direct and urgently delivered, while the stage direction supplies contextual intensity but not independent expressive/reflexive force.\",\"confidence\":1.0},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Immediately before it Christian says “Je t'aime !”; immediately afterward he says “Je t'adore !”\",\"supports\":\"The sequence establishes a rhetorical denial-and-replacement structure in which “adore” is offered as stronger than “love,” supporting dominant truth-conditional avowal force.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Roxane later tells him, “Vous m'aimez, je sais,” while criticizing his failed eloquence.\",\"supports\":\"Roxane interprets his underlying message as love; the dramatic conflict concerns how he expresses it, not whether he has just genuinely denied it.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is identified as negative present, with exact match “je ne t'aime pas.”\",\"supports\":\"Confirms that the surface form is a present-tense negation even though discourse context reverses its ordinary denial force through scalar correction.\",\"confidence\":1.0}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures both the sentence's assertion-like mode and its contextually recovered avowal of stronger love. The scalar correction and comic failure belong in contextual interpretation; they do not require O. Neither commitment force nor independently evidenced expressive discharge is present.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":\"The stage direction “vivement” and exclamation marks indicate urgency and emphasis only; under the calibrated E criterion they do not warrant E support.\"}"
        }
